On 2008-04-29, Mikael Lind <mikkelin@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Apr 29, 1:46 pm, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
>> This doesn't lend itself well to Nethack's play, especially from the
>> perspective of spell skills -- since a spellcaster may indeed not need
>> to use spells from a certain school.  As far as weapon skills go, it is
>> fairly rare for characters to swap out weapons, so the player won't see
>> any significant change from present.
> Can you clarify your comment about the spell skills? If the
> spellcaster does not use spells from a certain school, that skill
> would not be automatically advanced anyway. 

Spells in a given school are not necessarily used constantly throughout 
the game.  The divination spells are one such example; for some levels, 
keeping detect monster 'on' constantly is worthwhile.  For many others, 
it's just not necessary and the caster may wish to conserve his magic
points or spend them elsewhere.  Magic mapping is another good example;
it's highly prized in Gehennom and not really meaningful elsewhere, but
can only be cast usefully once per level.  A caster with only magic
mapping would need to find 'light' and spend his time constantly casting
it just to keep his skill up for that once-per-level MM; and now we get
into the 'victory dancing' problem.

(And what about spells such as polymorph?  There's little need to cast
it constantly, but if you don't, you might find yourself unable to --
and the low-level matter spells are not particularly useful in large
situations.)

> About the weapon skills, I
> want to allow players to switch weapons and have their weapon
> proficiency migrate to the new weapon over time.

You're missing the point.  There's generally little to no reason to
switch weapons right now except for specific 'immediate' cases; adding 
in elaborate code based around the player wanting to switch weapons doesn't 
mean people will actually bother doing it.

>> The other downside is that this encourages artificial play; ie. spend a
>> certain number of turns throwing daggers so your dagger skill doesn't go
>> down, then switch back to Frost Brand, then switch back to daggers...
>> etc.  This really isn't fun for the player, especially if the most
>> effective means of dealing with the problem is just to wade through the
>> critters.
> The hero would be able to remain Expert in several weapons as long as
> his experience level is high enough.

So until the player reaches that level, he's screwed?  That's terrible.

>> ("victory dancing" is already a problem in Nethack, so while this also
>> increases the player's tendency to do same, it isn't anything new.)
> I am not aware of this problem.

The concept of "skills degrade if not used" simply encourages the player 
to walk through the dungeon repeatedly using any skills he can manage on 
meaningless things in order to keep his levels high for when he wants to
use those skills -- in other words, victory dancing, where the player
performs a little dance every so often in order to achieve maximum
benefit from the game, subverting the intended design in the process.

